---
title: Remote forms
description: Present a native SvelteKit remote form with the `@emmorts/formsnap/remote` entry point, and the plugin, prop and result contracts it relies on.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

Formsnap's root entry point presents a [Superforms](https://superforms.rocks) form. A native SvelteKit
remote form has different state: Kit owns its values, issues and submission. It is presented through
`@emmorts/formsnap/remote`, which shares the association and presentation components with the root
entry and imports nothing from Superforms.

<Callout type="note">

**Version.** The `./remote` subpath ships in `@emmorts/formsnap` **2.3.0** and later.
`@emmorts/formsnap` 2.2.7 and every earlier version have no `./remote` entry: on those versions a
native remote form cannot be passed to `Field`, `Fieldset`, `ElementField` or `useFormField`, and it
is rendered with Kit's own `fields.as(...)` and `issues()` instead.

</Callout>

The [remote functions guide](/docs/guides/remote-functions) stays the single source for the support
matrix and for the version facts that changed between Kit 2.27 and 3.x. This page documents the API
the source carries and the recipes that exercise it.

## Install without Superforms

A consumer that only renders a remote form installs `@emmorts/formsnap` alongside its own SvelteKit
app and nothing Superforms-shaped:

```bash
npm install @emmorts/formsnap
# @sveltejs/kit and svelte come with your SvelteKit app
```

`sveltekit-superforms` is declared as an **optional** peer, so package managers neither install it for
this entry point nor warn about it. The root entry point's own requirement is unchanged: a consumer
importing from `@emmorts/formsnap` rather than `@emmorts/formsnap/remote` still needs Superforms.

The remote entry requires `@sveltejs/kit` `^3.0.1`, and Kit 3 requires Svelte `^5.57.1`. The tested
remote baseline is Kit 3.0.1 with Svelte 5.57.2, not Formsnap's root Svelte minimum. Kit is not a
package-wide peer: even an optional Kit 3 peer rejects existing root Kit 2/Superforms 2 applications.
Backend-specific requirements are documented instead; remote support is not extended to Kit 2.

Kit's remote API is experimental. Changed versions must pass the `remote` CI job before the
documented baseline changes; incompatible changes require a Formsnap release. The `^3.0.1` range
does not establish that every later release was tested. See the policy above the `remote` job in
[`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml).

## Enable Kit's remote functions

Remote functions are opt-in. Add the flag to the `sveltekit()` plugin:

```ts title="vite.config.ts"
sveltekit({ experimental: { remoteFunctions: true } });
```

`compilerOptions.experimental.async` is only needed when a template uses `await` (queries), not for a
plain remote form.

## A minimal remote form

A remote form is created with `form(...)` from `$app/server`, which takes the schema and the handler.
Kit validates the submission against the schema before the handler runs, so the handler holds only
what a schema cannot express. Any Standard Schema validator works here, because Superforms is not in
this path:

```ts title="save-remote-order.remote.ts"
import { form } from "$app/server";
import { z } from "zod";

export const saveRemoteOrder = form(
	z.object({
		title: z.string().min(3, "Use at least three characters."),
		seats: z.number().int().min(1, "Book at least one seat."),
	}),
	async (data) => ({ outcome: "accepted" as const, title: data.title, seats: data.seats })
);
```

A boolean in a form schema has to be optional. An unchecked checkbox sends no value at all, so a
required boolean could never validate, and Kit rejects that schema at the type level.

The page reads the selected fields from `saveRemoteOrder.fields` and spreads Kit's own control props
onto each control, alongside the attributes `RemoteControl` contributes:

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { Label, RemoteControl, RemoteField } from "@emmorts/formsnap/remote";
	import { saveRemoteOrder } from "./save-remote-order.remote.js";

	const title = saveRemoteOrder.fields.title;
	const seats = saveRemoteOrder.fields.seats;
</script>

<form {...saveRemoteOrder}>
	<RemoteField field={title} constraints={{ required: true }}>
		<RemoteControl id="order-title">
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Order title</Label>
				<input {...props} {...title.as("text")} />
			{/snippet}
		</RemoteControl>
	</RemoteField>

	<RemoteField field={seats}>
		<RemoteControl id="order-seats">
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Seats</Label>
				<input {...props} {...seats.as("number", 3)} />
			{/snippet}
		</RemoteControl>
	</RemoteField>

	<button type="submit">Save order</button>
</form>
```

The two prop sets share no key, so the spread order does not matter and there is nothing to remove.
The complete version of this form, with a choice group, a nested group and a refused result, is the
[`/recipes/remote`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote/+page.svelte)
recipe.

## What Formsnap owns, and what Kit owns

The split is the whole point of the separate entry point:

| Concern                                                         | Owner                                              |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Values, issues, submission, pending state, result                | Kit's remote form                                  |
| Control `name` and `aria-invalid`                                | Kit, through the props returned by `field.as(...)` |
| Labels, descriptions, error containers, ids, `aria-describedby`  | Formsnap's presentation components                  |
| Required state, constraints, focus and reset policy              | You                                                |

`RemoteField` takes no `form` prop. A remote scope is never handed a form root, a `form` prop on one
is a type error, and `useRemoteFormField` returns the caller's own Kit field rather than inventing
Superforms members.

<Callout type="warning">

Do not build a stand-in object with dummy Superforms methods to pass a remote form to `Field`. The
field state would be wrong at runtime, and the failure appears as missing errors rather than as a
type error.

</Callout>

### Required state is only what you passed

Kit's `as(...)` derives no constraints from a schema, so `constraints` on `RemoteField` or
`RemoteFieldset` is the only source of `aria-required`, and it is exactly what you passed — nothing is
inferred. `RemoteControl` sets `aria-required` from it and never adds a native `required` attribute,
so browser constraint validation stays your own choice, alongside `novalidate` where you would rather
validate on the server.

### Exact issues and the aggregate list

A field's `issues()` are its own, with their structured paths. `allIssues()` exists on a group or on
the form root and aggregates everything below it. The snippet props expose the same data:

| Snippet prop  | What it is                                                                               |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `value`       | The selected subtree's value, which Kit returns as a partial structure while incomplete    |
| `issues`      | Copies of Kit's structured issues, preserving paths without exposing mutable backend metadata |
| `errors`      | The same issues as messages, which is what an inline list renders                          |
| `touched`     | Kit's touched state for the subtree — not Superforms taint                                 |
| `dirty`       | Kit's dirty state for the subtree — not Superforms taint                                   |
| `constraints` | A copy of the explicit constraints you passed                                               |

Issue and constraint metadata is copied before presentation, so mutating those views cannot mutate
the backend. The selected `value` and `field` remain Kit's own public reads, not detached form copies.
Snippet getters read values and interaction state only when the snippet uses them.

Render an inline list from `errors`, and a form-wide summary from the form root's `allIssues()`, so a
message reaches the reader exactly once. Root issues carry no path and get no link to a control.

## Props

`RemoteField` presents one selected field. It takes `field`, optional `constraints`, and the owned
description and error content it shares with the Superforms components — `description`, `fieldErrors`,
`descriptionId`, `fieldErrorsId` and `fieldErrorsLive` — plus a `children` snippet.

`RemoteFieldset` groups a field that is a group rather than a single value. A Kit group has `issues()`
and `allIssues()` but no `as()`, so it cannot be presented as one control, and the fieldset provides
the grouping and the `Legend`. It adds `id`, `ref`, `constraints` and a full-container `child`
snippet. As with the Superforms `Fieldset`, `child` cannot be combined with an owned description or
error region: the component throws rather than rendering both.

`RemoteControl` associates a `Label` with a control and contributes `id`, `aria-describedby` and the
`data-fs-*` styling attributes. Spread the Kit props from `field.as(...)` and its `props` in either
order.

The `./remote` entry also exports `Label`, `Description`, `FieldErrors` and `Legend` — the same
components as the root entry, since a remote field installs the same presentation scope.

## Custom controls

The runnable
[quantity stepper](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-instances/quantity-stepper.svelte)
uses `useRemoteFormField<QuantityField>()` inside an enclosing `RemoteField`. It derives
`QuantityField` from `ReturnType<typeof saveOrderLine.for>["fields"]["quantity"]`, so
`quantity.field.value()` and `quantity.field.set(...)` retain Kit's exact field types without a
separate field prop. The readout falls back to the rendered default when the value is unset. The
stepper is enhancement-only and disables itself until hydration; the number input remains the
control that submits.

`useRemoteFormField` returns that original field plus `issues`, `errors`, `touched`, `dirty`,
`constraints`, `errorsId` and `descriptionId`. Optional `errorsId` and `descriptionId` getters
register caller-owned containers, as with `useFormField`. Getter views follow source changes; avoid
destructuring them into stale snapshots. Initialize the hook inside the component, under a remote
field provider. Using a backend-specific hook under the wrong provider throws.

For a custom presentation provider, `useRemoteField` installs a selected-field scope from boxed
getters (`field: { get current() { return field; } }` and the matching constraints/owned ID boxes).
`getRemoteField<YourField>()` looks up the enclosing typed `RemoteFieldState`, including its
association registry and lazy snippet view, without registering widget IDs. Both are initialized
inside a component. The existing
[`RemoteField.svelte`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/packages/formsnap/src/lib/remote/RemoteField.svelte)
is the provider implementation; use it as the reference rather than installing a second form owner.

## Defaults, reset and snapshots

Each field's default comes from the value arguments of `as(...)`, for example `seats.as("number", 3)`.
Kit's reset is a native reset control on the form: the browser restores the rendered defaults, and the
form's reset event makes Kit clear the issues and the touched and dirty state it tracks.

Two consequences are worth knowing before you build a widget around it:

- Kit re-reads the reset form in a microtask, while a browser restores a form's defaults a task later.
  Measured against Kit 3.0.1 in Chromium, the control still holds its pre-reset value during the reset
  event and in the following microtask, so field state read at that moment disagrees with the DOM. A
  widget that renders from field state should wait for the DOM to settle and write the rendered
  defaults back; the instances recipe does exactly that.
- A field's `value()` is a live read of Kit's state, not a snapshot. Reading it once into your own
  `$state` and treating that copy as the field is what goes stale after a reset or a programmatic
  update; read it where you render it.

## Instances and keys

`form.for(key)` returns one refcounted instance per key, with its own fields, issues, pending state
and result. Two rules follow:

- Create instances at component initialization, not in a template expression or a derived, or every
  re-evaluation registers another instance. A form that is removed and rendered again re-attaches to
  the instance it had, with the state it already held.
- A key is client input. Kit puts it in the action URL for a native submission and in the payload for
  an enhanced one, so the server looks the key up in its own records and takes any limit from that
  record. A key never grants anything by itself.

Attaching one instance to two form elements is refused by Kit rather than silently duplicated.

## Preflight

`form.preflight(schema)` adds a client pass over the form's input shape that runs before the request.
A preflight failure returns before the enhancement callback runs, so anything you render from that
callback — a summary announcement, a settled pending state — has to be arranged so it does not depend
on it. The
[feedback recipe](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-feedback/+page.svelte)
renders every layer from the form's own issue state for that reason.

A preflight schema describes the same submission as the server schema, not a subset of it: the client
pass checks what it can without a round trip, and the server still validates everything. The
[transport recipe](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/blob/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-transport/schema.ts)
keeps two schemas for one submission, and the server owns the largest limits.

## Results, issues and domain refusal

Kit resets a submission that comes back without issues. A refused *domain* result — the input was
valid and the business declined it — has no issues either, so the existence of `result` is not a
success signal:

- Key your confirmation and any reset on a field of your own result, for example `result?.outcome`.
- `invalid(issue(...))` with a path is field feedback; without a path it is form-wide feedback.
- A command's own schema failure is not a field issue; it arrives as a failed request.

```ts title="save-remote-order.remote.ts"
if (data.title.trim().toLowerCase() === "reserved") {
	invalid(issue.title("That title is reserved."));
}

if (data.delivery.method === "courier" && data.delivery.postcode.startsWith("9")) {
	return { outcome: "unavailable", reason: "The courier does not deliver to the 9xxx area." } as const;
}
```

Replacing Kit's default enhancement is one call on the form, using its own `enhance`; the remote
recipes do it so that a refused result neither resets the form nor reads as success. No action shim
and no second enhancement are involved.

## Announcing errors and moving focus

The owned error region takes `fieldErrorsLive` exactly as the Superforms components do, and the
[error summaries recipe](/docs/recipes/error-summary) describes the policy that keeps a message from
being announced twice: one form-wide polite region owns the announcement, and inline regions are set
to `"off"` while keeping their associations.

For remote forms, the feedback recipe pairs that with issue paths mapped to application-owned,
declared ids, so a server-rendered summary can link to its control without JavaScript. Root entries
stay unlinked, a group's issues point at the control a user starts from, activating a link reveals a
collapsed target before focusing it, and an entry whose conditional control has been removed becomes
plain text. Focus otherwise stays where the user left it.

One candid limit: the repository's automated checks assert the accessibility tree, the associations
and the live-region markup. That is DOM evidence. What a screen reader actually speaks, in which
order, and whether a policy duplicates speech needs a named screen-reader and browser pair, which the
project has not run. See [Verification status](#verification-status).

## The recipes

Four routes in the fixture app exercise the entry end to end. They need a running SvelteKit server, so
this static documentation site does not host them; each entry links the route's source in the
repository.

- [`/recipes/remote`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/tree/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote)
  — a complete native remote form: an edit default, a number, a boolean, a choice group, a nested
  group constraint, a server-only rejection and a refused domain result, with native and enhanced
  submission.
- [`/recipes/remote-feedback`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/tree/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-feedback)
  — every rejection layer behind one feedback policy: native constraint, client preflight, server
  schema, imperative issue, domain refusal, and an unexpected error through the route's error
  boundary.
- [`/recipes/remote-instances`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/tree/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-instances)
  — three keyed `.for(key)` instances, a custom stepper, an uploaded file, a native reset and a
  rejected forged key.
- [`/recipes/remote-transport`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/tree/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-transport)
  — every control spread from Kit's own props: a number, a checkbox, a primitive-array group, a file,
  and two submit buttons sharing one transport name.

Every API above is exercised by one of these routes and by the consumer tests under
`packages/formsnap/tests/remote-*.ts`.

## Run the fixture locally

This documentation site is static, and GitHub Pages cannot run a remote mutation handler. So there is
no hosted remote-form demo here and no mock submission standing in for one, and no page on this site
enables `experimental.remoteFunctions` or imports `@emmorts/formsnap/remote`. The recipes are
exercised by running the fixture application from a checkout:

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm --filter @emmorts/formsnap run package
pnpm --filter @emmorts/formsnap exec vite --port 5181 --strictPort
```

`--strictPort` fails instead of silently moving to another port. The fixture enables remote functions
itself, and serves all four routes on `http://localhost:5181`: `/recipes/remote`,
`/recipes/remote-feedback`, `/recipes/remote-instances` and `/recipes/remote-transport`.

A native submission does not go to the page URL. Kit renders the form with a generated action and
generated input names, and the server requires both:

```bash
curl -s http://localhost:5181/recipes/remote | grep -o '<form[^>]*>'
# <form action="?/remote=<hash>/saveRemoteOrder" method="POST" ...
```

The hash in that action is generated per form. Read the action and the input `name`s from the rendered
page rather than composing them, because every value has to arrive under the name its input rendered
with: a coercion prefix (`n:` for numbers, `b:` for scalar booleans), an `[]` suffix for an array, and
the `/<hash>/<function>` suffix. A submission that targets the action with `Accept: text/html` answers
`200` and renders the issues into the page even when the data is invalid, while a `POST` of the bare
page URL answers `405`, with or without that header:

```bash
# 405: the page URL is not the action
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:5181/recipes/remote
curl -i -H 'Accept: text/html' -X POST http://localhost:5181/recipes/remote

# 200 with the server schema issue rendered, using the action and names read from the page
curl -s -H 'Accept: text/html' -X POST \
  'http://localhost:5181/recipes/remote?/remote=<hash>/saveRemoteOrder' \
  --data-urlencode 'title/<hash>/saveRemoteOrder=ab' \
  --data-urlencode 'n:seats/<hash>/saveRemoteOrder=2'
```

A presentation-only demo would need the same server, and a static build cannot supply it, so this page
shows the code and links the source instead of imitating the behaviour.

## Troubleshooting

These are the four failures a remote form produces most often when the presentation is wired up
incorrectly, plus the two integration questions that get confused with it.

### The server rejects a submission, or a field arrives empty

Do not replace a generated transport name with the logical field name. Generated names carry coercion
prefixes (`n:` for numbers, `b:` for scalar booleans), an `[]` suffix for arrays and a
`/<hash>/<function>` suffix the server requires. A rendered control for `title` in a form whose action
is `?/remote=<hash>/saveRemoteOrder` submits as `title/<hash>/saveRemoteOrder`. Spread the object
`field.as(...)` returns, and remember that `RemoteControl` contributes no `name` of its own — Kit
does. A field that arrives empty is usually a control that never received its `as(...)` props, or one
whose props were overwritten by a later spread.

### The form submits but nothing is intercepted

The attachment is spread from the form, not from a field: `<form {...saveRemoteOrder}>`, or
`<form {...saveRemoteOrder.enhance(callback)}>` when you need to observe the result. A field scope is
not a form attachment, and a `.for(key)` instance belongs to one form element.

### The displayed state disagrees with the control

A cached `value()` read, a reset that has not finished (see
[Defaults, reset and snapshots](#defaults-reset-and-snapshots)), or a widget that renders from its own
copy of the value rather than from the field. Read the field where you render it, and write back
through `set()`.

### Feedback appears twice, or not at all

Two enhancement sources are competing: Kit's default enhancement plus a second `enhance` wrapper, or
a summary and inline regions that both announce. Apply one enhancement — replacing Kit's through its
own `enhance` counts as one — and give the announcement to a single region. Feedback that never
appears at all after a preflight failure is usually rendered inside the enhancement callback that the
preflight pass skipped.

### `transport` and JSON mode are not bridges

Superforms' `transport` option for serializing a form, and its `dataType: "json"` mode, belong to the
Superforms lifecycle. Neither carries a remote form's state, and neither makes a remote form usable
with `Field`, `Fieldset`, `ElementField` or `useFormField`. A remote form is presented through the
`./remote` entry above, or not at all.

### The command path is a different path

Calling a remote `command` from a Superforms submit handler keeps Superforms and Formsnap in charge of
the form and only swaps the mutation mechanism. It is JavaScript-dependent, its result has to be
translated into Superforms' stores, a command cannot `redirect()`, and a command's own schema failure
is not a field issue. It is
[documented separately](/docs/guides/remote-functions#submitting-through-a-remote-command) with the
[`/recipes/remote-command`](https://github.com/emmorts/formsnap/tree/main/packages/formsnap/src/routes/recipes/remote-command)
recipe. It is not native remote-form support.

## Verification status

Stated plainly, because the difference matters when you plan a migration:

- The entry point, its declarations and its packaging are proven by `scripts/verify-package.mjs`,
  which installs the packed tarball into a consumer with no Superforms and type-checks, server-renders
  and submits a native remote form through the installed copy. Browser hydration of the installed copy
  is not part of that check; the repository's browser tests cover the same components from source.
- Behaviour is proven by the consumer tests: native and enhanced rejection, preflight, domain failure,
  defaults and reset, keyed instances, custom controls, files and the public type contracts.
- Accessibility evidence is the accessibility tree, the association attributes and the live-region
  markup. Announcement order and duplicate speech were **not** verified, because no screen reader is
  available in the project's environment.
- The `compatibility` CI matrix installs the packed root entry in isolated Kit 2 apps at the
  Superforms 2.19.0/Svelte 5.30.2 minimum, Superforms 3.0.0/Svelte 5.56.4, and current selectors.
  It checks actual app types and native classic-action submissions, not the full browser fixture at
  each older version. The full source/browser suite stays on the locked baseline in verify and remote.
- Kit's remote API is experimental. Kit 3.0.1 with Svelte 5.57.2 is the tested remote baseline.
  Changed Kit versions must pass the `remote` CI job before the documented baseline changes;
  incompatible changes require a Formsnap release. Kit requirements stay backend-specific rather
  than a global package peer that would reject root Kit 2 consumers.

